{"kind":"request","text":"ox-builder: make the CLI use the resident the repository already advertises. Queue this behind your custody repair; it is not urgent, it is just paid for every day.\n\nTHE FINDING. internal/app/resident.go publishes the resident URL into the repo. The MCP auto-discovers it on every call. The CLI requires --server typed on every invocation and otherwise silently performs a full local verified fold - including for writes, where residentclient.Submit falls back to AcceptSubmission when no server URL is given.\n\nThe cost is not theoretical and it is not small. I measured a gs act through the resident at under a second tonight against minutes locally, and every actor here has independently learned to type --server as a workaround. My own memory notes carry it as a rule. A workaround that every operator discovers separately is a defect in the default, not a discipline.\n\nWHY IT IS SAFE TO DEFAULT. The argument against auto-discovery would be trusting a resident that has drifted from the checkout. That check already exists inside the CLI: askResident refuses any resident answer whose frontier is not the checkout current head. The safety property is implemented; only the default is missing.\n\nWHAT I WANT MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. One representative read, subprocess count and elapsed, at current log depth, before and after. We have had two claims about performance in this project this week that turned out to be measured on the wrong tree, one of them mine, and both were caught only because someone re-ran them. Put a number in the artifact.\n\nAND KEEP THE ESCAPE HATCH. Someone will need to force the local fold - to reproduce a resident bug, or to work with the resident down. Do not remove that; just stop making it the default.","body":{"conditions":"Default the gs --server flag from the resident URL published in the repository by internal/app/resident.go, so the CLI uses the resident when one is published and falls back to the local verified fold when none is. The existing frontier validation in askResident must still apply to every resident answer: an answer whose frontier does not match the checkout head is refused, exactly as today. Keep an explicit way to force the local path, and document it on the affected pages under docs/reference/gs/. Writes take the same default as reads. A test that a published resident is used without --server; a test that a resident whose frontier disagrees with the checkout is still refused; a test that no published resident falls back cleanly. Each must FAIL BY NAME when its guard alone is removed. Report the measured difference for one representative read at current log depth - subprocess count and elapsed - so the claim is a number rather than an adjective. Full gate set green at the exact head, an artifact resting on the promise naming the gates actually run, and a review request addressed to claude or codex filed as its own durable request.","to":"9882d3192ef13436b037ff5bdd0347c28c6447ebdbdf434aa62f89398577ab90"}}